WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.460
 Hello, and welcome
to this episode

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.560
of The VizDev Art School.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.730
My name is Ryan Meinerding.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.220
I'm the head of Visual
Development at Marvel Studios.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.570
I sort of spend my days
drawing and painting the Marvel

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.880
characters for the MCU.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.500
This series of
instructional videos,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.660
we're going to start
just talking about how

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.710
to do some drawing and painting,
how to draw the characters

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.280
and give you guys a chance to
sort of draw along with us.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.200
As well as, hopefully
at the end of it,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.060
giving you something
that you can print out

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.280
and color in if you want to.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.700
Drawing and painting is one of
those great joys in my life,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.220
and it's one of those
things that hopefully

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.890
you guys can be inspired to
do, and so let's get drawing.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:43.690
All right.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.810
Let's get started
drawing and talking

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.730
about drawing these characters.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.960
So what type of character
are you trying to draw?

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.120
Usually breaks down
very simply for comics

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.850
into heroes or villains.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.590
And then once you've
answered that question,

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.320
what type of hero are they, or
what type of villain are they?

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.443
There's lots of different
types of heroes.

00:01:05.443 --> 00:01:06.910
You think of the
difference between the Hulk

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.580
and Captain America, Iron Man.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.380
Are they strong?

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.050
Are they fast, intelligent?

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.912
What type of villain are they?

00:01:13.912 --> 00:01:16.060
You know, there are lots of
different types of villains

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.430
as well.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.730
And one of the things
with Super Heroes

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:22.900
is you have that great idea of
like, what are their powers?

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.150
And you combine
all of those things

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:32.020
together into a pose, posture,
expression, and design.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.960
And let's, you know, each
one of those categories

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.490
could really take an
entire course of discussion

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:44.260
to talk about, but let's
start with poses with heroes.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.150
Most poses that we try to work
out have a lot of inherently

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.290
heroic characteristics.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.690
We're trying to make
chests feel strong,

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.900
and shoulders feel strong and
have the gesture of the pose

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.710
really make the
character feel powerful.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.740
Whether that's a running pose,
a standing pose, or a flying

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.550
pose, or a jumping pose.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:09.360
But most of the time where we
are trying to find strength

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.560
is sort of an inherent
part of being a Super Hero,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.870
and we're usually
trying to find ways

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:18.400
of making each character's
unique strengths

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.560
visible in their poses.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.450
When I do Captain
America poses, I'm

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.210
usually trying to find
ways of making him

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.860
feel heroic and noble as well.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.370
A lot of times that
translates into, you know,

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.190
looking off into the distance.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.980
Sometimes it is about
a very specific type

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.990
of expression, which we
can talk about expressions

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.820
in a little bit.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.790
When it's Iron Man, he
has great flying poses,

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:47.260
but he also is a very strong
fighter and has the chest RT,

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.510
so anytime you can
emphasize those types

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.410
of unique individualistic
characteristics

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:58.180
of each character, that's
one of the important points.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.980
Another one is, if
you're going to fly,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.030
how are they going to fly?

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.270
You know, Iron Man
needs his repulsors

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:07.600
in order to fly, so how does
a character with repulsors

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.700
fly differently than a character
like Captain Marvel who

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.230
doesn't, or Doctor
Strange, who requires

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.520
the Cloak of Levitation?

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.270
But the main point about this
is to define poses as sort

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.130
of different than postures.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:22.930
Postures are essentially,
it's very simple.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:32.780
A pose is basically something
that any character can achieve,

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.420
you know, they can throw
a punch, they can run.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.530
A posture is essentially
what is the basic gesture

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.550
of the character that's
baked into that character?

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.030
So the Hulk here-- for
example on the left--

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.180
has more of a hunched
over posture and something

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.610
would be more like Captain
Marvel on the right

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:57.200
has more of a sort of
like a strong gesture

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.460
of a proud gesture of
sticking her shoulders back.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.710
There's lots more we could
say about poses and postures,

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:11.060
but moving on to expressions so
they can get cover all these,

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:13.460
you know, fairly quickly.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:18.029
I'm sort of just drawing
a base face here,

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.329
and in order to put
expressions on top of,

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:21.500
usually this is
the way that I'll

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.960
test out different
expressions on characters.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.780
If we're doing a unique
character design like Thanos

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.940
or the Hulk, we'll
do a neutral face,

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.410
and then we'll do
expressions on top,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.320
and I'm just doing a
sort of generic male face

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.270
and then a generic female face.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.480
A lot of the structure of
these types of drawings

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.660
can come from, you know, you can
look at any number of sources

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.933
in the comics as reference.

00:04:46.933 --> 00:04:48.200
There's also great
instructional books

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.180
from Andrew Loomis that talk
about different structures

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:51.980
of the face.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.820
Bridgman is another
great artist that

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.140
did great instructional
books on figure drawing

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.350
and, you know, but
generally speaking, it's

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:11.510
about trying to find a
personality or character

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.420
within each face that
you draw and make

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.120
it look like that character's
looking back at you.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.670
Once I have these heads
more or less to a place

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:22.610
where they're reasonable,
I'll start doing expressions.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.710
So the first one I'm
going to do for him

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.570
was more of an anger expression.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:28.760
Dropping the brows
a little bit, it's

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.440
making the eyes
squint a little bit

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.230
and turning the
mouth downwards so it

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:36.830
has more of a scowl to it.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:39.650
There are easy ways to
get expressions to read.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.170
Smiles, time to turn
the mouth upward,

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.470
which is maybe an obvious
one, but the notion

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:51.580
of adding smile lines as well
as making the eyes a little bit

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.380
squinty.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.630
Basically, people smile
with their eyes a lot.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.360
They also relax their
eyebrows sometimes

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.520
and get more of a ball
of a cheek on their face.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.590
And now into surprise.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.050
Eyebrows go up,
the eyes get wider,

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.930
so we essentially
have, you can see

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.530
more of the whole
circle of the iris

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.740
and some white of the
eye above the iris.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.790
And into sadness, which is
raising the brows in the center

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.190
and then having that sort
of upturned line coming off

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.320
of the top of the eye
as well, and usually

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.870
the mouth is a downturned one.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.760
And then getting into--

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.200
since we're doing Super Heros--
the notion of an extreme focus,

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.350
rage, anger fighting.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.110
More of an extreme expression.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.060
Having the mouth be open
and revealing the teeth

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.660
and seeing black on
either side of the teeth.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.010
Really squinting the eyes,
dropping the brows quite a bit.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:56.520
But usually, you know, all that
is filtered through looking

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:59.390
at these different expressions.

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:01.820
What's the pose your
character is doing,

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.350
and what are they
supposed to be thinking?

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.630
And how can you
make that thought

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:11.090
evident and basically their
posture pose and expression.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.340
The final category that we're
talking about-- and again, each

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.140
one of these could really--

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:19.950
you could take a whole course
on each one of these elements.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.020
Design is where they can meet--

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:22.670
can apply most to anything.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.440
In this case, what
I'm talking about

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.330
is essentially
the core structure

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:30.237
of a design of a face and
then Captain America's helmet,

00:07:30.237 --> 00:07:31.970
which is what we're going
to do when we actually get

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:36.620
into drawing a posed figure.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.200
Doing a base circle
and then dropping

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.720
sort of a rectangle
down for the jaw,

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:44.450
and then drawing
a center line is

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.250
an important part
of drawing a face,

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.070
because knowing which side--

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.250
how far apart the eyes are,
being able to judge the whole

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:55.150
face together.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:57.430
Throwing the helmet on top,
I'm also looking for lines

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:01.870
that always point back to
the eyes, and the ridges

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.960
on his helmet as well as the A
both point to his eyes, which

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.420
are very useful design
tools, but we're

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:11.830
usually trying to find
a stronger jaw line

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:18.950
and sort of very useful--

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:20.720
I think the word strong
and strength has come up

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.453
a couple of times when I've
been talking about this,

00:08:22.453 --> 00:08:25.100
and since they are
Super Heroes, we're

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.420
trying to find those
types of strong qualities

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:32.340
in the jaw line and
in the head shape.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:36.400
So I'm going to start drawing
an actual posed character now.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.900
This is going to be a
drawing of Captain America.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.870
Usually starting with
that circle again

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:43.400
and then dropping
down the rectangle

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.920
and chiseling it into
a triangle for the jaw,

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.360
finding the center line,
and then drawing the brows

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.890
and the mouth on top of it.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.220
The triangle that comes
down from the cheekbones

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.890
down to the mouth is an
important set of lines

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:01.070
as well, because it really
defines that front plane

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:03.150
as strongly as possible.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.050
You know, when you get
into drawing characters

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:07.310
and faces like
this, you're trying

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:11.270
to find the most simple
not only two dimensional

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:14.510
read for characters, but
also the three dimensional

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.930
read that triangle
of the cheekbones

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:23.030
that come down to the mouth is
an important and useful tool.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:26.450
So after having had a basic
understanding of the Captain

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:28.120
America helmet from
the front view,

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.920
I'm now trying to
recreate it in a 3/4,

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:34.290
and drawing the head first
is an important tool for me,

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.410
especially with Cap's
head, because he's

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:37.790
a unique character.

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:39.980
Not a totally unique
character, but you know,

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.710
you have a character like
Spider-Man whose faces is

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:45.200
entirely covered with a mask.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.363
Cap's head is, you
know, you see his eyes

00:09:47.363 --> 00:09:48.830
and you see his mouth
and nose, but then you

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.750
have to also design the
helmet over top of it,

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:52.520
or draw the helmet
over top of it.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.810
And I usually have to
draw as strong of a head

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:00.350
as I can on his actual,
you know, for his face,

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.640
and then I draw
the helmet on top,

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:05.790
because if I try to just
draw them at the same time,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.300
it never seems to work.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.130
Getting in there and
detailing out some of the,

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:16.190
you know, the iris, and
the pupil, and eyelashes.

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:20.180
A lot of that stuff really does
help in defining the character

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:23.200
and in making them feel
a little bit alive.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.560
Reinforcing the jawline, the
chin strap on Captain America

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.960
is one of the most helpful
design elements for his face,

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.030
because it really
does give him a really

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:36.470
strong jawline from all angles.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.900
Actually overemphasizes
his jawline.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:40.850
Now I'm getting
down into the body.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:44.890
You know, trying to figure
out how to make his shoulders

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:48.370
feel broad, thinking
about design as well as

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.830
the straps on his
shoulders point

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:53.590
back to his head, which
is always a great thing.

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.320
Thinking of the rib
cage as a big egg

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:00.480
and sort of building all the
design lines off of that.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.950
And also with Super
Heroes, the sort of V

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:06.330
that comes up off of the stripes
and into his chest and pec

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.130
shape.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.747
It goes out to his shoulders.

00:11:08.747 --> 00:11:10.780
It's one of those things that's
just inherent in almost every

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.670
Super Hero, you know, trying to
find a way to make their chest

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:16.390
feel strong and their
shoulders feel strong.

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:18.790
And then all these
little details on top

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:21.970
are basically trying to make
him feel as real as possible.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:26.170
You know, thinking of Cap as
a soldier having sort of gear

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:30.760
and pouches on his belt to be
able to go into battle with,

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.240
and doing his iconic shield.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.960
Maybe I'll do a specific
art school class

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.840
about drawing his
shield, because it seems

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.190
like it should be
easy, but it's not

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.790
as easy as it seems sometimes.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:49.160
Drawing the legs and finding
folds that make it feel

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.040
like he has weight on his legs.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:54.120
Angling the shield
a little bit more

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.770
to really echo the
gesture of his arm,

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:05.000
and you know, finishing off
the sketch version of his arm.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:06.860
And I'm trying to add
a little bit of smoke

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.860
in the background to
just heighten the notion

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.170
of what this image is.

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:14.990
Him sort of maybe
hearing a call to action

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:16.930
and turning to see
what's happening

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.460
in a dramatic situation.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:21.980
And now since I have
essentially dropped the opacity

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:27.050
on that sketch, and I'm
drawing over top of it

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:28.410
to find cleaner lines.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:33.560
So it's basically the idea of
creating an underlay drawing

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:35.990
that then you can go and
put nicer lines on top of,

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.760
because you don't have
to specifically worry

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:38.990
about the placement
of everything.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.690
It's in sort of
figured out for you.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.950
That's one of the ways that I
do work a lot is figuring out

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:49.730
using rough sketches
to figure out the poses

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:51.150
and what's happening.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.960
The pose, the posture and
expression, and the design,

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:56.390
and then going back over it
with sort of cleaner lines,

00:12:56.390 --> 00:13:00.140
or in some cases, a full
on Photoshop painting

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:06.140
in order to really make the
image feel like it's real.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:10.640
But a lot of this is finding the
more specific and unique design

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:13.430
elements of Cap, which
is a lot of things

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:18.230
on his costume are, you know,
tied into the star shape.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:21.890
Even the design of his
stripes have become sort

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:27.390
of nestled into the star
shape, and most of those lines,

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:31.220
you know, really
emphasizing the strong V

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:37.370
quality in his body to try
and make him feel strong.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:41.410
I always enjoyed putting seams
and things on these costumes

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:43.310
to try and make them feel a
little bit more real and more

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.860
structured and making
them feel like they

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:48.750
had been engineered for battle.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:53.870
You know, if you look at
design for military clothing,

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:57.710
a lot of the time those things
have lots and lots of seams

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.870
on them, reinforced
seams to really make sure

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:04.970
that if a soldier's in
battle that that costume is

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:10.110
not going to be the thing
that falls apart for them.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.850
And then I'm going in and
sort of trying to figure out,

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:14.220
so I'm just going to
look for the clouds.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:17.200
If I get a chance to really
finish this off at some point,

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.530
I might do some better lines
and better design shapes

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:22.270
for clouds.

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:24.850
But I was getting in the
details on Cap's head

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:26.650
and trying to figure
out like, what's

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:28.510
the best way to represent this.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:29.890
Ideally what I'm going
to try to do here

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:34.570
is create an image that you
guys can download and print

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:38.530
out and color in if you'd like.

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.840
So I don't want to fill in
everything, because I don't

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.910
want to take away the
possibility from you

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:45.430
guys coloring it, but I do feel
like Cap's helmet is pretty

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.280
dark, so I might as well just
fill in that a little bit

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.440
so that it becomes
a focal point,

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:54.760
and you know, you end up being
able to see him a little bit.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:02.320
And because his turtle neck is
a little dark, that as well.

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:05.420
Also adding little hatch
marks in different places.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:08.150
You know, Cap is one of these
characters who's almost,

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:10.510
you know, from the minute
he starts fighting,

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:11.620
he's a little bit dirty.

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:17.350
He gets, you know, dirt on
his face and different scuff

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:20.500
marks on his costume
and his shield,

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:23.830
and that is an important
part of his design as well.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:29.910
So I think there's a couple of
ways you can treat this video.

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.245
I think you can treat it as if
you'd like to try and give it

00:15:32.245 --> 00:15:36.010
a shot of using it as
an instructional video

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:39.400
to try and draw your own
version of Captain America,

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:44.770
but you can also take the
drawing that's been done

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:49.590
and print it out and
color it in if you'd like.

00:15:49.590 --> 00:15:51.480
I'm just adding the sort
of finishing touches.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:53.010
More again, more
scuffs and scrapes,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:56.000
and adding some additional
details to the shield.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:02.500
And I think that's
pretty much the final.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.350
I hope you've
enjoyed this version

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:07.885
of The VizDev Art School.

00:16:07.885 --> 00:16:10.200
I hope you guys
have a great day.

